<spell-notebook "tree-diagram usage">
	<header level="1">
		Tree diagram
		<header>A tree diagram draws a tree top-down:  a box for each node, a line to each of its children.</>
	</>
	<tree-diagram-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<html>
			<ui-menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
				<ui-item link="#using-it">Using it</>
				<ui-item link="#from-script">From script</>
				<ui-item link="#size">Size</>
				<ui-item link="#keyboard">Keyboard</>
				<ui-item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
			</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  the data contract, the two ways to give it, sizing, framework snippets, accessibility. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			A tree diagram draws any tree from plain data: it knows nothing about where the tree came from. Each
			node is a `TreeDiagramNode` (exported by `spell-ui`):

			| Field | What it is |
			|---|---|
			| `label` | The box's text, e.g. `If`, `Variable number`, `Number 15`. Required. |
			| `detail` | A second, smaller, muted line under the label, e.g. a datatype `number`. |
			| `slot` | Text on the line from its parent, e.g. `condition`, `body`, `otherwise`. |
			| `title` | Hover text on its box, e.g. the source it came from. |
			| `children` | Its children, left to right. |

			Give the tree one of two ways:

			- The `tree` property, from script or a framework. It redraws whenever `tree` is set again: set a NEW
			  tree, since a tree changed in place isn't seen.
			- A `<script type="application/json">` child holding the tree as JSON, for a static page with no script
			  of its own (it works from `file://` too). The diagram follows the child if the page changes it.

			The property wins when both are there; setting it back to `undefined` shows the child's tree again. With
			no tree, or JSON that doesn't parse, it draws nothing and takes no room; bad JSON also logs one console
			warning. Data is checked, not trusted: a node without a `label` gets an empty box, and anything that
			isn't a node is left out.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```html
			{ "label": "Add", "children": [{ "label": "Number 1" }, { "label": "Number 2" }] }
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="From script">
		<markdown>
			Set `tree` on the element. In Solid, pass it as a plain prop, `tree={...}`:  the element takes the
			object whole, as its property.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			import "spell/ui/ui-tree-diagram"

			const diagram = document.querySelector("ui-tree-diagram")
			diagram.tree = {
			  label: "If",
			  children: [
			    { label: "IsA · integer", slot: "condition", detail: "boolean" },
			    { label: "Print", slot: "body" }
			  ]
			}
			```
		</>
		<markdown>
			```tsx
			import type { TreeDiagramNode } from "spell/ui"

			function ParseTree(props: { tree: TreeDiagramNode }) {
			  return <ui-tree-diagram tree={props.tree} />
			}
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Size">
		<markdown>
			A tree is laid out tidy: each depth a row, each parent centred over its children, no two boxes or
			slot labels overlapping. Boxes are sized to their text, in a monospace font.

			It draws at its natural size, `--ui-tree-diagram-font-size` (13px in 16px text) for labels, and never
			larger. In a narrower column it shrinks to fit, down to `--ui-tree-diagram-min-scale` (three quarters);
			a tree wider than that scrolls sideways inside the element instead of shrinking past readable.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>
			Nothing in a tree diagram takes focus: it's a picture. A tree wide enough to scroll scrolls with the
			scrollbar or a trackpad; browsers that make scrolling boxes focusable also let the arrow keys scroll it.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- The drawing is one `<svg role="img">`, named by a summary of its root: "Tree: If, with 3 children"
			  (translatable).
			- A node's `title` is its box's `<title>`: hover text for a pointer.
			- The summary doesn't read out the whole tree. Where its shape matters to a reader, say it in the
			  text around the diagram too.
			- If drawing fails, the element shows the tree as nested lists instead, which a screen reader reads in
			  full.
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
